              "inline_data": null,
              "text": "**\"Still in Love with You\"** is a song by the legendary country music duo **Brooks & Dunn**, featured as the closing track (Track 10) on their landmark 1991 debut studio album, ***Brand New Man***. \n\nHere are the key details and interesting facts about this classic neotraditional country ballad:\n\n### 1. Songwriters and Production\nThe song was co-written by both members of the duo, **Kix Brooks** and **Ronnie Dunn**, alongside their frequent collaborator and producer **Don Cook**. It was co-produced by Cook and Scott Hendricks.\n\n### 2. Kix Brooks on Lead Vocals\nWhile Ronnie Dunn sang lead vocals on the majority of the duo's biggest radio hits (such as \"Neon Moon\" and \"Believe\"), **Kix Brooks takes the lead vocal spotlight** on \"Still in Love with You\". His performance highlights his softer, earnest vocal style, backed by Ronnie Dunn and Dennis Wilson on background vocals.\n\n### 3. Theme & Lyrics\nThe track is a bittersweet, melancholic ballad about the painful departure of a lover. Despite trying to move on, the narrator realizes that distance and time cannot change how they feel. \n\nThe opening verse sets the scene of a hard goodbye:\n> *My taxi cab is waiting*\n> *Wish I could hold you one last time*\n> *After all we've had together*\n> *There's gotta be a better way of telling you goodbye*\n\nThe soaring chorus uses classic country imagery to express the impossibility of getting over the relationship:\n> *If I had a horse, I'd ride off in the sunset*\n> *If I had wings, I'd fly off in the sky so blue*\n> *If I had the time, I'd spend my whole life dreaming*\n> *But I'd wake up, still in love with you*\n\n### 4. Album Context\nThough it was never released as a radio single (the five singles from the album were \"Brand New Man\", \"My Next Broken Heart\", \"Neon Moon\", \"Boot Scootin' Boogie\", and \"Lost and Found\"), \"Still in Love with You\" remains a beloved deep cut among fans of early 90s country. It helped round out *Brand New Man*, which went on to be certified quadruple-platinum and established Brooks & Dunn as the most successful duo in country music history.",
